      "claim": "Acamprosate, a small highly polar ionized amide/sulfonate, has absolute oral bioavailability of only ~11% in humans under fasting conditions; its high transfer score and shared ionized polar/amide character make it the strongest direct evidence that the query's extreme polarity will also yield F <20%.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C13"],
      "claim": "Ferulic acid, an α,β-unsaturated carboxylic acid with polar phenolic functionality, shows only 3–10% oral bioavailability in human studies; the shared cinnamic-acid-like carboxylate and polar groups support low F for the query.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C12"],
      "claim": "Tegaserod has absolute oral bioavailability of ~10% in fasted humans; despite being basic rather than acidic, its consistently low F in the same assay context supports that the query, with even more extreme polarity, will also be poorly absorbed.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C33"],
      "claim": "Microcystin-LR, a cyclic peptide with multiple polar ionizable groups, is reported to have low oral bioavailability in humans; although transfer is limited, it reinforces that peptide-like macrocycles with high polarity are poorly absorbed orally.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "decision_summary": "The query is an extremely polar, fully ionized dicarboxylic acid/cyclic peptide with very low predicted permeability. The most transferable experimental evidence—acamprosate (F≈11%), ferulic acid (F 3–10%), and tegaserod (F≈10%)—all show low oral bioavailability in humans. High-bioavailability comparators (arginine, amoxicillin, finasteride, MMF, vinorelbine) have very low transferability and lack the query's key physicochemical barriers. Therefore, F is predicted to be below 20%.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct experimental pharmacokinetic data exist for the query molecule.",
    "No permeability (e.g., Caco-2) or transporter-mediated uptake/efflux data are available.",
    "No formulation, prodrug, or food-effect data are available.",
    "A single acamprosate record reporting 42% bioavailability is an outlier relative to multiple IV-comparison studies showing ~11%, creating minor uncertainty.",
    "Cyclic peptide conformation could theoretically alter permeability, but the extreme polarity likely dominates."
  ],
